\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx1440\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\tab Elf\tab Dex +1, Con -1
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx1440\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 \tab Gnome\tab Int +1, Wis -1
\par \tab Half-elf\tab no adjustments
\par \tab Halfling\tab Dex +1, Str -1
\par \tab Human\tab no adjustments
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Consult tables 1-6 and record the various bonuses and penalties the character receives for having particularly high or low scores.
\par \tab Consult the racial descriptions in chapter 2 and record the character's special racial abilities.
\par \tab Finally, check Tables 10, 11, and 12 to determine the character's height, weight, starting age, and age effects.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Step 3: Select a Class (chapter 3)}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx1440\adjustright {\fs24 \tab Select a class that is available to your character's race:
\par \tab Dwarf\tab F, C, T, F/T, F/C
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx1440\outlinelevel0\adjustright {\tab Elf\tab F, R, M, C, T, F/M, F/T, M/T, F/M/T
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx1440\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 \tab Gnome\tab F, I, C, T, F/C, F/I, F/T, C/I, C/T, I/T
\par \tab Half-elf\tab F, R, M, C, D, T, B, F/C, F/T, F/D, F/M, C/R, C/M, T/M, F/M/C, F/M/T
\par \tab Halfling\tab F, C, T, F/T
\par \tab Human\tab F, P, R, M, I, C, D, T, B
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Check Table 13 for class-based ability score restrictions. Read the class description and record special class abilities and restrictions.
\par \tab If your character is a fighter, paladin, or ranger, is not a halfling, and has a Strength score of 18, roll d100 to determine exceptional Strength. Consult Table 1 and readjust those bonuses affected by exceptional Strength.
\par \tab If your character is a mage, consult Table 4 and record his maximum spell level, chance to learn spells, and maximum number of spells per level. Ask your DM what spells the character knows.
\par \tab If your character is a cleric, consult Table 5 and record bonus spells and his chance of spell failure. Note the spell spheres to which the PC has access.
\par \tab If your character is a thief, record his base thieving skills scores from Table 26. Modify these scores according to Tables 27 and 28. Then apportion 60 points between those abilities, assigning no more than 30 points to any one score.
\par \tab If your character is a bard, not his thief abilities from Table 33. Modify these percentages according to Tables 27 and 28. Then apportion 20 points between these abilities.
