\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Chapter 1:
\par }{\b\fs48\cf6 Player Character Ability Scores 
\par }{\fs24 
\par To venture into the worlds of the AD&D game, you first need to create a }{\i\fs24 character.}{\fs24 
 The character you create is your alter ego in the fantasy realms of this game, a make-believe person who is under your control and through whom you vicariously explore the world the Dungeon Master (DM) has created.
\par \tab Each c
haracter in the AD&D game has six abilities: Strength, Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma. The first three abilities represent the physical nature of the character, while the second three quantify his mental and personality traits
.
\par \tab In various places throughout these rules, the following abbreviations are used for the ability names: Strength--Str; Dexterity--Dex; Constitution--Con; Intelligence--Int; Wisdom--Wis; Charisma--Cha.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 Rolling Ability Scores 
\par }{\fs24 Let's first see how to generate ability scores for your character, after which definitions of each ability will be given.
\par \tab The six ability scores are determined randomly by rolling six-sided dice to obtain a score from 3 to 18. There are several methods for rolling up these scores.
\par }{\b\fs24  \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method I:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
Roll three six-sided dice (3d6); the total shown on the dice is your character's Strength ability score. Repeat this for Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, and Chrisma, in that order. This method gives a range of scores from 3 to 18, w
ith most results in the 9 to 12 range. Only a few characters have high scores (15 and above), so you should treasure these characters.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Alternative Dice-Rolling Methods 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Method I creates characters whose ability scores are usually between 9 and 12. If you
 would rather play a character of truly heroic proportions, ask your DM if he allows players to use optional methods for rolling up characters. These optional methods are designed to produce above-average characters.
\par }{\b\fs24  \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method II}{\fs24\cf6 : }{\fs24 Roll 3d6 twice, noting the
 total of each roll. Use whichever result you prefer for your character's Strength score. Repeat this for Dexterity, Constitution, Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma. This allows you to pick the best score from each pair, generally ensuring that your char
acter does not have any really low ability scores (but low ability scores are not all that bad any way!).
\par }{\b\fs24  \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method III:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Roll 3d6 six times and jot down the total for each roll. Assign the scores to your character's six abilities however you want. This gives
 you the chance to custom-tailor your character, although you are not guaranteed high scores.
\par }{\b\fs24  \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method IV:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Roll 3d6 twelve times and jot down all twelve totals. Choose six of these rolls (generally the six best rolls) and assign them to your character's ab
ilities however you want. This combines the best of methods II and III, but takes somewhat longer.
\par \tab As an example, Joan rolls 3d6 twelve times and gets results of 12, 5, 6, 8, 10, 15, 9, 12, 6, 11, 10, and 7. She chooses the six best rolls (15, 12, 12, 11,
 10, and 10) and then assigns them to her character's abilities so as to create the strengths and weaknesses that she wants her character to have (see the ability descriptions following this section for explanations of the abilities).
\par }{\b\fs24  \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method V:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Roll four
 six-sided dice (4d6). Discard the lowest die and total the remaining three. Repeat this five more times, then assign the six numbers to the character's abilities however you want. This is a fast method that gives you a good character, but you can still g
et low scores (after all, you could roll 1s on all four dice!).
\par }{\b\fs24  \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Method VI:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This method can be used if you want to create a specific type of character. It does not guarantee that you will get the character you want, but it will improve your chances.
\par \tab 
Each ability starts with a score of 8. Then roll seven dice. These dice can be added to your character's abilities as you wish. All the points on a die must be added to the same ability score. For example, if a 6 is rolled on one die, all 6 points must be
 assigned to one ability. You can add as many dice as you want to any ability, but no ability score can exceed 18 points. If you cannot make an 18 by exact count on the dice, you cannot have an 18 score.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 The Ability Scores 
\par }{\fs24 The six character abilities are 
described below. Each description gives an idea of what that ability encompasses. Specific game effects are also given. At the end of each ability description is the table giving all modifiers and game information for each ability score. The blue-shaded a
bility scores can be obtained only by extraordinary means, whether by good fortune (finding a magical book that raises a score) or ill fortune (an attack by a creature that lowers a score).
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Strength 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Strength (Str) measures a character's muscle, endurance
, and stamina. This ability is the prime requisite of warriors because they must be physically powerful in order to wear armor and wield heavy weapons. A fighter with a score of 16 or more in Strength gains a 10% bonus to the experience points he earns.

\par \tab F
urthermore, any warrior with a Strength score of 18 is entitled to roll percentile dice (see Glossary) to determine exceptional Strength; exceptional Strength improves the character's chance to hit an enemy, increases the damage he causes with each hit, i
ncreases the weight the character is able to carry without a penalty for encumbrance (see below), and increases the character's ability to force open doors and similar portals.
\par \tab The rest of this section on Strength consists of explanations of the columns in Table 1. Refer to the table as you read.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Hit Probability}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 adjustments are added to or subtracted from the attack roll rolled on 1d20 (one 20-sided die) during combat. A bonus (positive number) makes the opponent easier to hit; a penalty (negative number) 
makes him harder to hit.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Damage Adjustment}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
also applies to combat. The listed number is added to or subtracted from the dice rolled to determine the damage caused by an attack (regardless of subtractions, a successful attack roll can never cause less than
 1 point of damage). For example, a short sword normally causes 1d6 points of damage (a range of 1 to 6). An attacker with Strength 17 causes one extra point of damage, for a range of 2 to 7 points of damage. The damage adjustment also applies to missile 
weapons, although bows must be specially made to gain the bonus; crossbows never benefit from the user's Strength.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Weight Allowance}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 is the weight (in pounds) a character can carry without being encumbered (encumbrance measures how a character's possessions
 hamper his movement--see Glossary). These weights are expressed in pounds. A character carrying up to the listed weight can move his full movement rate.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Maximum Press}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 is the heaviest weight a character can pick up and lift over his head. A character cann
ot walk more than a few steps this way. No human or humanoid creature without exceptional Strength can lift more than twice his body weight over his head. In 1987, the world record for lifting a weight overhead in a single move was 465 pounds. A heroic fi
ghter with Strength 18/00 (see Table 1) can lift up to 480 pounds the same way and he can hold it overhead for a longer time!
\par }{\b\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Open Doors}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 indicates the character's chance to force open a heavy or stu
ck door. When a character tries to force a door open, roll 1d20. If the result is equal to or less than the listed number, the door opens. A character can keep trying to open a door until it finally opens, but each attempt takes time (exactly how much is 
up to the DM) and makes a lot of noise.
\par \tab Numbers in parentheses are the chances (on 1d20) to open a locked, barred, or magically held door, but only one attempt per door can ever be made. If it fails, no further attempts by that character can succeed.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Bend Bars/Lift Gates}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
states the character's percentage chance (rolled on percentile dice) to bend normal, soft iron bars, lift a vertical gate (portcullis), or perform a similar feat of enormous strength. When the character makes the attempt, roll percentile d
ice. If the number rolled is equal to or less than the number listed on Table 1, the character bends the bar or lifts the gate. If the attempt fails, the character can never succeed at that task. A character can, however, try to bend the bars on a gate th
at he couldn't lift, and vice versa.
\par 
