\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 Calculating THAC0 
\par }{\fs24 \tab To make an attack roll, the character's THAC0 must be known. This depends on the group
 and level, if the attacker is a player character or NPC, or the Hit Dice if the attacker is a monster or an animal. All 1st-level characters have THAC0s of 20, regardless of class.
\par \tab For a character of level 1 through level 20, consult Table 53. This table lists the THAC0 number of each group through 20th level, so players don't have to perform any calculations.
\par \tab For a character higher than 20th level, find the Improvement Rate for the character's group in Table 54. There you'll find the number of levels a 
character must advance to reduce his THAC0 by 1 (or more) points. Calculate the character's THAC0 according to his level.
\par \tab The DMG contains the information on monster THAC0s.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 Combat and Encounters 
\par }{\fs24 Encounters are the heart of the AD&D game. Since encounte
rs with monsters and NPCs often lead to combat, an understanding of what happens during battles is vital for everyone. There are several factors the DM will consider in any combat, most of which arise from the circumstances of the encounter. Is anyone sur
prised? How far apart are the opponents? How many of them are there? Answers to these questions are found in the Encounter section of the DMG. These are questions common to all encounters, whether combat occurs or not.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 The Combat Round 
\par }{\fs24 If an encounter escalates into a combat situation, the time scale of the game automatically goes to }{\i\fs24 rounds}{\fs24 
 (also called melee rounds or combat rounds). Rounds are used to measure the actions of characters in combat (or other intensive actions in which time is important).
\par \tab A round is approximately one minute long. Ten combat rounds equal a }{\i\fs24 turn}{\fs24  (or, put another way, a turn equals 10 minutes of game time). This is particularly important to remember for spells that last for turns, rather than rounds.

\par \tab But these are just approxim
ations--precise time measurements are impossible to make in combat. An action that might be ridiculously easy under normal circumstances could become an undertaking of truly heroic scale when attempted in the middle of a furious, chaotic battle.
\par \tab Imagine the simple act of imbibing a healing potion. First, a character decides to drink the potion before retiring for the night. All he has to do is get it out of his backpack, uncork it, and drink the contents. No problem.
\par \tab Now imagine the same thing in the midd
le of a fight. The potion is safely stowed in the character's backpack. First, he takes stock of the situation to see if anyone else can get the potion out for him, but, not surprisingly, everyone is rather busy. So, sword in one hand, he shrugs one strap
 of the pack off his shoulder. Then, just as two orcs leap toward him, the other strap threatens to slip down, entangling his sword arm. Already the loose strap keeps him from fully using his shield.
\par \tab Holding the shield as best as possible in front of him, 
he scrambles backward to avoid the monsters' first wild swings. He gets pushed back a few more feet when a companion shoulders past to block their advance. His companion bought him a little time, so he kneels, lays down his sword, and slips the backpack a
ll the way off. Hearing a wild cry, he instinctively swings his shield up just in time to ward off a glancing blow.
\par \tab Rummaging through the pack, he finally finds the potion, pulls it out, and, huddling behind his shield, works the cork free. Just then there
 is a flash of flame all around him--a fireball! He grits his teeth against the heat, shock, and pain and tries to remember not to crush or spill the potion vial. Biting back the pain of the flames, he is relieved to see the potion is still intact.
\par \tab Quickl
y, he gulps it down, reclaims his sword, kicks his backpack out of the way, and runs back up to the front line. In game terms, the character withdrew, was missed by one attacker, made a successful saving throw vs. spell (from the fireball), drank a potion
, and was ready for combat the next round.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 What You Can Do in One Round 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Whatever the precise length of a combat round, a character can accomplish only one basic action in that round, be it making an attack, casting a spell, drinking a potion, or tending 
to a fallen comrade. The basic action, however, may involve several lesser actions.
\par \tab When making an attack, a character is likely to close with his opponent, circle for an opening, feint here, jab there, block a thrust, leap back, and perhaps finally make 
a telling blow. A spellcaster may fumble for his components, dodge an attacker, mentally review the steps of the spell, intone the spell, and then move to safety when it is all done. It has already been shown what drinking a potion might entail. All of th
ese things might happen in a bit less than a minute or a bit more, but the standard is one minute and one action to the round.
\par \tab Some examples of the actions a character can accomplish include the following:
\par  Make an attack (make attack rolls up to the maximum number allowed the character class at a given level)
\par  \tab \bullet  Cast one spell (if the casting time is one round or less)
\par  \tab \bullet  Drink a potion
\par  \tab \bullet  Light a torch
\par  \tab \bullet  Use a magical item
\par  \tab \bullet  Move to the limit of his movement rate
\par  \tab \bullet  Attempt to open a stuck or secret door
\par  \tab \bullet  Bind a character's wounds
\par  \tab \bullet  Search a body
\par  \tab \bullet  Hammer in a spike
\par  \tab \bullet  Recover a dropped weapon
\par 
\par \tab There are also actions that take a negligible amount of time, things the character does without affecting his ability to perform a more important task. Examples of these include the following:
\par  \bullet  Shout warnings, brief instructions, or demands for surrender, but not conversations where a reply is expected.
\par  \bullet  Change weapons by dropping one and drawing another.
\par  \bullet  Drop excess equipment, such as backpacks, lanterns, or torches.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 The Combat Sequence 
\par }\pard\plain \s16\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {In real life, combat is one of the closest things to pure anarchy. Each side is attempting to harm the other, essentially causing disorder and chaos. Thus, combats are filled w
ith unknowns--unplanned events, failed attacks, lack of communication, and general confusion and uncertainty. However, to play a battle in the game, it is necessary to impose some order on the actions that occur. Within a combat round, there is a set seri
es of steps that must be followed. These steps are:
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par \tab 1.\tab The DM decides what actions the monsters or NPCs will take, including casting spells (if any).
\par \tab 2.\tab The players indicate what their characters will do, including casting spells (if any).
\par \tab 3.\tab Initiative is determined.
\par \tab 4.\tab Attacks are made in order of initiative.
\par 
\par \tab These steps are followed until the combat ends--either one side is defeated, surrenders, or runs away.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 NPC/Monster Determination:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 In the first step, the DM secretly decides in general terms wh
at each opponent will do--attack, flee, or cast a spell. He does not announce his decisions to the players. If a spell is to be cast, the DM picks the spell before the players announce their characters' actions.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Player Determination:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Next, the players giv
e a general indication of what their characters are planning to do. This does not have to be perfectly precise and can be changed somewhat, if the DM decides circumstances warrant.
\par \tab 
If the characters are battling goblins, a player can say, "My fighter will attack" without having to announce which goblin he will strike. If the characters are battling a mixed group of goblins and ogres, the player has to state whether his character is 
attacking goblins or ogres.
\par \tab Spells to be cast must also be announced at this time and cannot be changed once the initiative die is rolled.
\par \tab Before moving on, the DM will make sure he has a clear idea of not only what the player characters are doing, but al
so what actions any hirelings and henchmen are taking. Once he has a clear view of everything that's likely to happen, the DM can overrule any announced action that violates the rules (or in the case of an NPC, is out of character).
\par \tab He is not required to 
overrule an impossible action, but he can let a character attempt it anyway, knowing full well the character cannot succeed. It is not the DM's position to advise players on the best strategies, most intelligent actions, or optimum maneuvers for their cha
racters.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Initiative:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 In the third step, dice are rolled to determine initiative, according to the rules for initiative (see "Initiative" below).
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Resolution:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 In the last step, PCs, NPCs, and monsters make their attacks, spells occur, and any other actions are resolved according to the order of initiative.
\par \tab The above sequence is not immutable. Indeed, some monsters violate the standard sequence, and some situations demand the application of common sense. In these cases the DM's word is final.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Rath is leading a party through the corridors of a dungeon. Right behind him are Rupert and Delsenora. Rounding a bend, they see a group of orcs and trolls about 20 feet away. No one is surprised by the encounter.
\par \tab The DM has notes telling him the orcs are hesitant. He
 secretly decides that they will fall back and let the trolls fight. The trolls, able to regenerate, are naturally overconfident and step forward to the front rank (cursing the orcs at the same time) and prepare to attack. Turning to the players, the DM a
sks, "What are you going to do?"
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\i\fs24 Harry (playing Rath, a dwarf who hates orcs): "Orcs?--CHARGE!"
\par Anne (playing Delsenora the wizard): "Uh--what!? Wait--don't do that . . . I was going to . . . now I can't use a fireball."
\par DM: "Rath is charging forward. Quick--what are you doing?"
\par Jon (playing Rupert, the half-elf, to Anne): "Cast a spell! (To DM) Can I fire my bow over him?"
\par DM: "Sure, he's short."
\par Jon: "OK, I'll shoot at orcs."
\par DM: "Anne, tell me what Delsenora's doing or she'll lose the round trying to make up her mind!"
\par Anne: "Got it!--Acid arrow spell at the lead troll."
\par DM: "Fine. Harry, Rath is in front. Roll for initiative."
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 Initiative 
\par }{\fs24 \tab The initiative roll determines who acts first in any given combat round. Initiative is not set, but changes from ro
und to round (combat being an uncertain thing, at best). A character never knows for certain if he will get to act before another.
\par \tab Initiative is normally determined with a single roll for each side in a conflict. This tells whether all the members of the group get to act before or after those of the other side(s).
\par \tab There are also two optional methods that can be used to determine initiative. Each of these optional methods breaks the group action down into more individual initiatives. However, the general m
ethod of determining initiative remains the same in all cases.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Standard Initiative Procedure 
\par }{\fs24 \tab To determi
ne the initiative order for a round of combat, roll 1d10 for each side in the battle. Normally, this means the DM rolls for the monsters (or NPCs), while one of the players rolls for the PC party. Low roll wins initiative. If more than two sides are invol
ved in combat, the remaining sides act in ascending order of initiative.
\par \tab If both (or all) sides roll the same number for initiative, everything happens simultaneously--all attack rolls, damage, spells, and other actions are completed before any results are
 applied. It is possible for a wizard to be slain by goblins who collapse from his }{\i\fs24 sleep}{\fs24  spell at the end of the round.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Initiative Modifiers 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Situational factors can affect who has initiative. To reflect this, modifiers are added to or subtracted from the initiative die roll.
\par 
