\par Area of Effect: sphere with diameter\tab Saving Throw: Neg.
\par of 1 ft./level
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell is exactly the same as the 4th-level wizard spell }{\i\fs24 Otiluke's resilient sphere,}{\fs24  with the addition that the creatures or objects inside the globe are nearly we
ightless--anything contained within it weighs only 1/16 its normal weight. Any subject weighing up to 5,000 pounds can be telekinetically lifted in the sphere by the caster. Range of control extends to a maximum distance of 10 yards per level after the sp
h
ere has actually succeeded in encapsulating a subject or subjects. Note that even if more than 5,000 pounds of weight is englobed, the perceived weight is only 1/16 of the actual weight, so the orb can be rolled without exceptional effort. Because of the 
r
educed weight, rapid motion or falling within the field of the sphere is relatively harmless to the object therein, although it can be disastrous should the globe disappear when the subject inside is high above a hard surface. The caster can dismiss the e
ffect with a word.
\par \tab In addition to a hemispherical piece of diamond and a matching piece of gum arabic, the caster must also have a pair of small bar magnets as material components for this spell.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs28\cf6 Otto's Irresistible Dance }{\b\fs24\cf6 
\par (Enchantment/Charm)}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx3600\adjustright {\fs24 Range: Touch\tab Components: V
\par Duration: 1d4+1 rounds\tab Casting Time: 5
\par Area of Effect: Creature touched\tab Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab When an }{\i\fs24 Otto's irresistible dance}{\fs24  spell is placed upon a creature, the spell causes the recipient to begin dancing, complete with feet shuffling an
d tapping. This dance makes it impossible for the victim to do anything other than caper and prance; this cavorting worsens the Armor Class of the creature by -4, makes saving throws impossible except on a roll of 20, and negates any consideration of a sh
ield. Note that the creature must be touched, as if melee combat were taking place and the spellcaster were striking to do damage.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs28\cf6 Permanency }{\b\fs24\cf6 
\par (Alteration)}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx3600\adjustright {\fs24 Range: Special\tab Components: V, S
\par Duration: Permanent\tab Casting Time: 2 rds.
\par Area of Effect: Special\tab Saving Throw: None
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab This spell affects the duration of certain other spells, making the duration permanent. The personal spells upon which a }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  is known to be effective are as follows:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx3600\adjustright {\i\fs24 comprehend languages\tab protection from evil
\par detect evil\tab protection from normal missiles
\par detect invisibility\tab read magic
\par detect magic\tab tongues
\par infravision\tab unseen servant
\par protection from cantrips}{\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab The wizard casts the desired spell and then follows it with the }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  spell. Each }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  spell lowers the wizard's C
onstitution by 1 point. The wizard cannot cast these spells upon other creatures. This application of permanency can be dispelled only by a wizard of greater level than the spellcaster was when he cast the spell.
\par \tab In addition to personal use, the }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  spell can be used to make the following object/creature or area-effect spells permanent:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2160\adjustright {\i\fs24 enlarge\tab prismatic sphere
\par fear\tab stinking cloud
\par gust of wind\tab wall of fire
\par invisibility\tab wall of force
\par magic mouth\tab web}{\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Additionally, the following spells can be cast upon objects or areas only and rendered permanent:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2160\adjustright {\i\fs24 alarm\tab wall of fire
\par audible glamer\tab distance distortion
\par dancing lights\tab teleport
\par solid fog
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab These applications to other spells allow it to be cast simultaneously with any of the latter when no living creature is the target, but the entire spell complex then can be dispelled normally, and thus negated.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 permanency}{\fs24  spell is also used in the fabrication of magical items (see the 6th-level spell }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24 ). At the DM's option, permanency might 
become unstable or fail after a long period of at least 1,000 years. Unstable effects might operate intermittently or fail altogether.
\par \tab The DM may allow other selected spells to be made permanent. Researching this possible application of a spell costs as m
uch time and money as independently researching the selected spell. If the DM has already determined that the application is not possible, the research automatically fails. Note that the wizard never learns what is possible except by the success or failur
e of his research.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs28\cf6 Polymorph Any Object }{\b\fs24\cf6 
\par (Alteration)}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2880\adjustright {\fs24 Range: 5 yds./level\tab Components: V, S, M
\par Duration: Variable\tab Casting Time: 1 rd.
\par Area of Effect: Special\tab Saving Throw: Special
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2880\adjustright {\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 \tab This spell changes one object or creature into another. When used as a }{\i\fs24 polymorph other}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh}{\fs24 
 spell, simply treat the spell as a more powerful version, with saving throws made with -4 penalties to the die roll. When it is cast in order to change other objects, the duration of the spell depends on how radical a change 
is made from the original state to its enchanted state, as well as how different it is in size. The DM determines the changes by using the following guidelines:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2160\adjustright {\i\fs24 Kingdom}{\fs24 \tab Animal, vegetable, mineral
\par }{\i\fs24 Class}{\fs24 \tab Mammals, bipeds, fungi, metals, etc.
\par }{\i\fs24 Relationship\tab }{\fs24 Twig is to tree, sand is to beach, etc.
\par }{\i\fs24 Size}{\fs24 \tab Smaller, equal, larger
\par }{\i\fs24 Shape}{\fs24 \tab Comparative resemblance of the original to the polymorphed state
\par }{\i\fs24 Intelligence}{\fs24 \tab Particularly with regard to a change in which the end product is 
\par \tab more intelligent
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab A change in }{\i\fs24 kingdom}{\fs24 
 makes the spell work for hours (if removed by one kingdom) or turns (if removed by two). Other changes likewise affect spell duration. Thus, changing a lion to an androsphinx would be permanent, but turning a turnip to a purple worm would be a chan
ge with a duration measured in hours. Turning a tusk into an elephant would be permanent, but turning a twig into a sword would be a change with a duration of several turns.
\par \tab All polymorphed objects radiate a strong magic, and if a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  spell is successfully cast upon them, they return to their natural form. Note that a }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh}{\fs24 
 spell or its reverse will affect objects under this spell. As with other polymorph spells, damage sustained in the new form can result in the injury or death of the polymorphed creature.
\par \tab For example, it is possible to polymorph a creature into rock and grind it to dust, causing damage, perhaps even death. If the creature was changed to dust to start with, more creative methods to damage it would be needed; perhaps the 
wizard could use a }{\i\fs24 gust of wind}{\fs24  spell to scatter the dust far and wide. In general, damage occurs when the new form is altered through physical force, although the DM will have to adjudicate many of these situations.
\par \tab The system shock roll must be applied to living creatures, as must the restrictions noted regarding the }{\i\fs24 polymorph other}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh}{\fs24 
 spells. Also note that a polymorph effect often detracts from an item's or creature's powers, but does not add new powers, except possibly movement capabilities not present in the old form. Thus, a }{\i\fs24 vorpal sword}{\fs24 
 polymorphed into a dagger would not retain vorpal capability. Likewise, valueless items cannot be made into permanent valuable items.
\par \tab The material components of this spell are mercury, gum arabic, and smoke.
\par 
